  "body": "<p>Clive joins Vessalia, Thorn and Velkyn on the grain caravan. His caution about the attackers remains part of the encounter, alongside the consequences of using force while others try to talk.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clive-caravan\">Protecting the caravan</h2>\n<p>He questions the merchant about the attacks and tells a rider to shelter if wounded, offering to drive. He also objects when Papirak is referred to disrespectfully. These are actions and reactions in the scene, not a complete earlier history.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scene references: <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562136738506215546\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562137132917588060\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562163322177061006\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">3</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clive-parley\">A mistimed attack</h2>\n<p>When the slasher realizes he faces adventurers and becomes willing to listen, Clive attacks his mind while demanding surrender. The narrator explicitly says this spoils that opening for peace. Vessalia later restores a conversation through persuasion and healing, then silences Clive while negotiating.</p><p>The outcome is neither proof that caution was needless nor that negotiation was impossible. The party still obtains a conditional pause in raids.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scene references: <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562180689913319497\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562181724656377958\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562182059596709991\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">3</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562184235631251637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">4</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562190420321112116\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">5</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562190864833323049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">6</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clive-doubt\">A risk left open</h2>\n<p>Clive asks whether the proposed meeting could be a trap and points to the wounds the raiders inflicted. The source closes before the meeting with the First Hunter. Its safety, the caravan’s later delivery and any lasting settlement remain unknown.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Scene references: <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562189570521563207\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562190445130289223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2</a>, <a href=\"https://discord.com/channels/1177270231513694249/1199083630354501673/1562190639607709797\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">3</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clive-service-and-choice\">What service should ask of a person</h2>\n<p>Clive brings stone to Ryvyting Creations and speaks with Farkur about his fear of losing control in a fight. His account of his powers remains personal testimony. Farkur urges preparation and service, without demonstrating that the feared loss of control has occurred.</p><p>Later, Pell challenges Clive’s assumption that earlier arrivals can expect a newcomer to fight. Pell insists on being asked and being able to choose. Their disagreement does not create a compulsory rule of the Gate. Clive also helps Pell practice with weapons and later takes up cleaning at the bathhouse: his own service includes work away from combat.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clive-learning-trust\">Trust, and the people who question it</h2><p>Clive tells Willford that he is trying to prove himself to people who treat him as a nuisance. Willford asks whether he has actually spoken to them about their reasons. He has not. Clive accepts the correction, apologizing for deciding what others think before asking them.</p><p>On the road to Eovar with Derek Belmont, he argues for trusting people even when it is risky. Derek presses the cost: what if that trust puts someone Clive loves in danger? Clive promises to protect them, even at the cost of his life. Derek calls it a tall claim. His generosity is real, but another traveler has asked whether conviction alone can keep the promise.</p><h2 id=\"clive-volunteers-and-roofs\">A request for soldiers becomes work for refugees</h2><p>Melian will let Clive seek volunteers to defend Pristinia, but will not let him force civilians into service. Her questions turn his attention from a future military emergency toward the people who might need somewhere to sleep. He promises to help with refuge for Dasa.</p><p>When Tshuka finds him with plans and material lists, he is still preparing permanent lodgings. She answers with towers conjured on the spot, offering blankets, rooms and a bath. Clive admires what she can do while worrying about the repeated casting a temporary solution will require. His permanent building has not been completed, but the conversation now has roofs standing beside it.</p><h2 id=\"clive-vale-investigation\">Anger in an investigation about love</h2><p>In the Vale investigation, Clive wants to help a frightened wife but is quickly angry with the husband who appears to have abandoned her. He proposes confrontation and warns himself to stay calm while the company follows Elias. The others keep investigating instead of accepting his first explanation.</p><p>The later encounter at the memorial leaves the cause unresolved. Clive’s wish to protect Mara has not yet told him what Nhalis is or how Elias’s obsession began. <a href=\"#vale-household-investigation\">The full investigation</a> preserves his concern beside the company’s slower, incomplete search for an answer.</p><h2 id=\"clive-fire-and-worth\">Wanting fire to do something kinder</h2><p>At Aurélia’s observatory, Clive wishes his flames could heal people rather than only harm them. Thorn tells him of druids who use fire differently. He wants to learn, without having yet met such a teacher or gained that power.</p><p>When their talk turns toward being odd, he admits a fear of being discarded unless he is perfect. Thorn tells him there is nothing wrong with being odd. The knight who makes large promises for other people is also asking, less directly, whether he can belong while still unfinished.</p>",
